t some may need to break the fast at a very specific minute with dates and water, others might be flexible to break a few minutes later if a meeting runs over. Their energy schedules might differ. Think of it like this: you’ve observed that they all take the rules seriously, but *how* that seriousness translates into practical logistics can still vary. And assuming you already know the specifics—rather than asking—can accidentally put someone in an awkward spot if their practice doesn't match your well-intentioned plan. For instance, you might set aside a prayer space for "the strict Muslims" only to find one person can't use it because it has pictures on the wall, or you chose a vegetarian meal that still contains an alcohol-based sauce they avoid. So the core advice doesn't change, but the framing can: **Precisely because they are strict, asking them directly becomes even more important.** You're not trying to discover if they're strict—they clearly are—but you're asking which logistics let them follow those rules comfortably, without them having to negotiate on the fly. You can still send a note that acknowledges the gravity: "I know prayer, dietary needs, and other observances are important to you, and I want to make sure the retreat setup genuinely works. Could you let me know what you'd need regarding prayer breaks/space, specific food requirements (even beyond halal/no pork—any hidden ingredients?), and any other details I might miss? I'm happy to make it seamless." This respects that they take their practice seriously and positions you as someone who wants to get the details right—not someone assuming they're a monolith. If you've truly seen the exact same behavior from every single person, it's still statistically wise to check. One day you'll meet a Muslim who is just as strict on prayer but has a shellfish allergy cross-contamination concern you'd never imagine, or who only eats Zabiha halal and won't touch anything else. A quick private conversation prevents that outlier from becoming an unintentional offense.
